{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"computer use\" \"using computers\" electronics appliances) (+ \"greenhouse gas emissions\" \"carbon dioxide\" \"global warming\") (+ \"extreme weather\" hurricanes floods droughts wildfires))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_1283944748#3_2036309832","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2226","rank":2,"snippet":"resources? \" , \"segment\": \"Evaporation is why the “air” is so refreshing at the seashore in spite of the FACT very little vegetation is present - same thing in the middle of the ocean. If trees converted carbon dioxide into oxygen it would be easier to breathe in a high-altitude forest - and how would CO2 “know” where forests were located - such as the billions of tons released into the atmosphere during the recent CA <cover>wildfires? Does carbon dioxide go up, then sidewise and down to forests? And there are SO many other rational questions not answered by this absolutely inane belief. Yep! Oxygen is a renewable resource. Dan O'Brien , B.Sc, M.Sc, Ph. D Physics, Electronics (</cover>1980) Answered 2 years ago · Author has 1.7K answers and 767.2K answer views I take it you are familiar with the oxygen cycle?. Oxygen is consumed by all living creatures,including plants and usually results in the production of co2 and water ,and the release of energy. How the chlorophyll molecules in green leaves and grasses in the presence of sunlight can convert CO2 back into molecular oxygen,and build up cellulose structures in plants.\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1098012319#7_1978264759","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
