{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_440662621#27_598895783","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage gives general global-warming solution tips and says Energy Star appliances and clean filters save energy and reduce carbon dioxide. It is related to household energy use and emissions, but it is not about electronics use causing natural disasters.","learned":"Energy-saving advice pages can support that appliances/electrical use affect CO2 emissions, but they often lack the electronics focus and natural-disaster link."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2234","reason":"This query is retrieving substantive energy-waste and emissions material involving appliances/electronics; continue to see if later results more directly connect electronics/computer energy use to global warming or disasters."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2234"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1489181295#0_2125111476","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2234","rank":2,"snippet":"warming. HVAC System Heating and cooling your home uses energy in the form of electricity or natural gas. Much of the electricity in the United States is produced by burning coal or oil, which releases greenhouse gases into the atmosphere (see References 2). Your air conditioner also contains refrigerants that, if allowed to escape into the atmosphere, contribute heavily to global warming and may additionally deplete the ozone layer, depending on the age of the unit (see References 3). Appliances Like your HVAC system, appliances in your home use electricity that causes greenhouse gas emissions. Some appliances like refrigerators and freezers also use refrigerants. The Environmental Protection Agency recommends replacing old appliances with Energy Star-qualified models to <cover>save electricity and reduce greenhouse emissions (see References 2). Using existing appliances and other electronic devices more efficiently can also help lower electricity and natural gas consumption in a household (see References 4).\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Things in Your House That Cause Global Warming | </cover>Home Guides | SF Gate\" , \"url\": \"http://homeguides.sfgate.com/things-house-cause-global-warming-78827.html\" }"}}
